{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>So Moses wrote this song.</b> It is the Song of Ha'azinu. <br><b>On that day.</b> It is possible that this was the day of his death, for he did not delay. And the word <b>and He charged</b> — את יהושע בן נון — returns to the Holy One, blessed be He; and this is what is meant by 'I have sworn,' and likewise He said, 'and I will be with you'; and it is an explanation of what the Exalted One promised (above, verse 14): ואצונו — 'and I will charge him.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The words 'נשבעתי' ('I have sworn') and 'ואנכי אהיה עמך' ('and I will be with you') appear as unmarked biblical quotations; the latter recalls HaShem's assurance to Joshua (e.g. Deuteronomy 31:23, Joshua 1:5), while 'נשבעתי' likely alludes to the divine oath regarding Joshua's commission. R. Bahya cites these phrases without full scriptural citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:50.857634113Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}